User accounts and UUID identity in backup collection/restore
============================================================

A site-specific backup includes only active users in the requested organization
with an explicit grant to that active site. Unrestricted organization accounts
and users assigned only to other sites are excluded. An organization-wide
backup remains the way to carry all organization accounts. include_users=false
still excludes both users and their access grants.

Previously a site backup included every organization user but only grants for
the selected site. The real PostgreSQL probe demonstrates that restoring one
unrelated restricted user could recreate it with zero grants. CurrentUser treats
zero grants as unrestricted site access, so this was a privilege expansion.
The corrected collection reconciles the actual exported user/grant sets after
both reads: no selected user without a matching grant and no new grant without
its selected user is emitted. Tests inject grant/lifecycle changes between SQL
reads; they are not a cross-process concurrency or caller-revocation test.

For users belonging to multiple sites, a site snapshot intentionally carries
only the selected site's grants. It is a partial snapshot, not a full account
recovery artifact. Whole-organization lifecycle inconsistencies and previously
created archives are unchanged; this fix does not retroactively certify them.

The core restore now converts JSON UUID strings to Python UUID objects for
mapped UUID columns before lookup/insertion/update. Bulk INSERT RETURNING uses
typed identities to correlate records; relying on PostgreSQL's implicit string
conversion caused actual bulk restoration to fail. Invalid UUID values are
counted as skipped without poisoning the transaction. JSON fields containing
UUID-shaped text remain JSON strings. Existing tenant, grant-target, secret-field
and overwrite rules are preserved. No database schema or dependency change is
required. Module-specific restore helpers are outside this change.

Qualification covers actual PostgreSQL user collection and core user/grant
restoration, read-cap preservation, typed bulk site/credential/automation
inserts, malformed identifiers, and encrypted local archive write/readback.
The independent probe characterizes the original bulk failure and successful
single-user privilege expansion, then checks the repaired behavior. It does
not exercise HTTP authentication, first-install recovery, every contributor,
device configuration restore, remote storage durability or customer RTO/RPO.
Core credential scope and other contributor scope still need separate review.

